or-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 78,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n          \"Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 139,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-009\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenue: **$89.023 billion** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: not stored | article 46\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 46,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-010\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenue growth: **117%** | basis: year-over-year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 37\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 37,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n          \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n          \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-011\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenues: **$89.02 billion** | basis: up 117% year over year; segment | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: second quarter of fiscal 2027 | said: not stored | article 92\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 92,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-012\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data center segment revenue: **$89 billion** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 73: basis: Segment; roughly 92.5% of total revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) | said: not stored || article 122: basis: Data Center segment, up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 73,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\n          \"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 122,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.\",\n          \"Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-013\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center segment revenues: **$89 billion** | basis: Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 35\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 35,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-033\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"Data center revenue: **$89 billion** | basis: Data center segment, up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal second quarter | said: not stored | article 22\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 22,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion,\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-041\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"data center revenue: **$89 billion** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 114: basis: quarterly data center segment revenue, up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored || article 116: basis: data center segment, latest quarter | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 114,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"According to the latest prints, Nvidia's quarterly data center revenue is now $89 billion -- up 117% year over year.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 116,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n          \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-042\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"Data-center revenue: **$89 billion** | basis: Data-center segment revenue, as reported in the company's quarterly results | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored | article 13\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 13,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-043\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"Data-center share of total sales: **roughly 92.5%** | basis: Data-center revenue divided by total revenue, as calculated by GuruFocus | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored | article 13\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 13,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-045\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter (unspecified)\",\n       \"line\": \"Data center revenue: **$89 billion** | basis: Data center segment revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Latest reported quarter (unspecified) | said: not stored | article 39\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 39,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-046\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter (unspecified)\",\n       \"line\": \"Data center share of revenue: **roughly 92.5%** | basis: Data center revenue as a share of total revenue (approximately) | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Latest reported quarter (unspecified) | said: not stored | article 39\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 39,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-056\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > reported quarter (not named in the item)\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenue: **$89.0 billion** | basis: rising 117% | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: Reported quarter (not named in the item) | said: not stored | article 74\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 74,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) generated $96.2 billion of quarterly revenue, up 106%, with Data Center revenue rising 117% to $89.0 billion and gross margin at 75%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-067\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > the quarter (period not further specified in the item)\",\n       \"line\": \"data center sales: **$89B** | basis: Data center segment | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: The quarter (period not further specified in the item) | said: not stored | article 95\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 95,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA (NVDA) posted $96.2B revenue; data center sales were $89B, about 92.5% of total — key revenue driver for the quarter.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-068\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > the quarter (period not further specified in the item)\",\n       \"line\": \"data center share of total revenue: **about 92.5% of total** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: The quarter (period not further specified in the item) | said: not stored | article 95\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 95,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA (NVDA) posted $96.2B revenue; data center sales were $89B, about 92.5% of total — key revenue driver for the quarter.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue $178 billion (company-wide; up 96% year over year)\",\n     \"period\": \"First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported (section: Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual); source as stored: Nvidia)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-029\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > first half of fiscal 2027 (ended july 26, 2026)\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue: **$178 billion** | basis: Company-wide revenue, up 96% year over year | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026) | said: not stored | article 105\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 105,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For now, Nvidia remains primarily focused on AI. In the first half of its fiscal 2027 (which ended July 26), its revenue rose by 96% year over year to $178 billion. Also, due to the relatively slower growth in its costs and expenses, its net income surged by 161% to $118 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue $215.9 billion (T-037 stores 'close to $215.9 billion'); up roughly 65.5% year over year; company total (the last full fiscal year reported)\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026) in T-001; stored as 'Fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026' in T-037, T-038\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported (section: Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual); source as stored: NVIDIA / Nvidia)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-001\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2026\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue: **$215.9 billion** | basis: company total; up roughly 65.5% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2026 (ended January 2026) | said: not stored | article 116\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 116,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In fiscal 2026, which ended in January 2026, revenue reached nearly $215.9 billion, up roughly 65.5% year over year. This scale produced net income of approximately $120.1 billion. The net margin remained remarkably high at approximately 55.6%, consistent with the 55.8% net margin achieved in the previous fiscal year.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-037\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > fiscal year ended jan. 25, 2026\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue: **close to $215.9 billion** | basis: Company, fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026 | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: Fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026 | said: not stored | article 229\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 229,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In the fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a massive increase of approximately 65.5% year over year. Net income for the period was nearly $120.1 billion, producing a net margin of roughly 55.6%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-038\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > fiscal year ended jan. 25, 2026\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue growth, year over year: **approximately 65.5%** | basis: Company, fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026 | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: Fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026 | said: not stored | article 229\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 229,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In the fiscal year ended Jan. 25, 2026, revenue reached close to $215.9 billion, a massive increase of approximately 65.5% year over year. Net income for the period was nearly $120.1 billion, producing a net margin of roughly 55.6%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ],\n   \"S4\": [\n    {\n     \"group\": \"a\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue growth 70%, approximately 70%, about 70%, or roughly 70%\",\n     \"period\": \"fiscal 2028; Fiscal 2028; Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA management (CEO) / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / Nvidia (Colette Kress, CFO) / NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress), company outlook\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-093\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue growth: **70%** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 16: basis: company projection made with second-quarter earnings; compared with 45% analysts had expected at the time | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored || article 201: basis: Company projection for the next year, made with second-quarter results | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: about 1 month ago\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 16,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 201,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028)\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-094\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue growth: **about 70%** | stored from 4 articles, each with its own details: || article 43: basis: Nvidia's own guidance as reported by Barchart; growth measure not further specified in the item | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: Last month || article 92: basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored || article 98: basis: Full-year company revenue growth guidance; first time NVIDIA has guided a full year ahead | source as stored: NVIDIA management (CEO) | period as stored: Fiscal 2028 | said: not stored || article 177: basis: preliminary outlook; company-wide; management described it as supply-constrained | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 43,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Last month, Nvidia posted a market-beating Q2 and issued bullish guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which makes its current P/E multiple of about 24x appear rather attractive.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 92,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 98,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 177,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Management’s preliminary outlook is for revenue to grow about 70% in fiscal 2028. Management called that outlook supply-constrained. Demand without that limit would be a lot higher, management said.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-095\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue growth: **roughly 70%** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 121: basis: Company outlook stated by CFO on the fiscal Q2 earnings call; described as supply-constrained | source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028) | said: late August 2026 || article 222: basis: not stored | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 222,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"The lower multiple comes despite Nvidia forecasting roughly 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 as demand for AI training and inference continues to drive its business.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-096\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue growth expectation: **approximately 70%** | basis: Preliminary; year-over-year growth | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored | article 54\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 54,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%.\",\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-097\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue growth guidance: **70%** | basis: Company guidance | source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: Fiscal 2028 | said: not stored | article 22\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 22,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-098\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue growth guidance: **approximately 70%** | basis: supply-constrained outlook | source as stored: Nvidia (Colette Kress, CFO) | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: late August 2026 (Nvidia earnings call) | article 53\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 53,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"On the company's earnings call in late August, chief financial officer Colette Kress went a whole year further.\",\n          \"\\\"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\\\" Kress said. \\\"This is a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-099\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"sales growth: **70%** | basis: company-wide forecast by management in the fiscal 2027 second-quarter report; company says it is supply-constrained and that growth could double if commodity supply constraints were overcome | source as stored: NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: fiscal 2028 | said: not stored | article 45\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 45,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips.\",\n          \"But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware.\",\n          \"On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 2027 earnings call , Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said:\",\n          \"Incredibly, we are seeing demand acceleration even at our scale. Customer forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply-constrained.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"a\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue growth roughly 70%, around 70%, or 70%\",\n     \"period\": \"next fiscal year; next year\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA / Nvidia, company outlook\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-105\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > next fiscal year\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue growth: **roughly 70%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: next fiscal year | said: not stored | article 55\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 55,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-106\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > next fiscal year\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue growth guidance: **around 70%** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: next fiscal year | said: not stored | article 166\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 166,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-108\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own outlook (guidance) > next year\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue growth forecast: **70%** | basis: Nvidia's projection that current GPU supply/demand conditions continue; company-level revenue growth | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: next year | said: not stored | article 162\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 162,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia projects this supply/demand situation will continue, contributing to a 70% revenue growth forecast for next year.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue $671.95 billion; growth 65.5%\",\n     \"period\": \"Next fiscal year\",\n     \"whose\": \"Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimate)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-134\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > next fiscal year\",\n       \"line\": \"Consensus sales estimate: **$671.95 billion** | basis: Consensus revenue, versus current year (+65.5%), as reported by Zacks | source as stored: Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimate) | period as stored: Next fiscal year | said: 2026-09-22 | article 11\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 11,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion and $671.95 billion estimates indicate +88% and +65.5% changes, respectively.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue growth 66%\",\n     \"period\": \"Next year (fiscal 2028, ends January 2028)\",\n     \"whose\": \"Wall Street consensus (analysts)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-139\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > next year (fiscal 2028, ends january 2028)\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue growth: **66%** | basis: Wall Street consensus expectation; company-wide revenue growth | source as stored: Wall Street consensus (analysts) | period as stored: Next year (fiscal 2028, ends January 2028) | said: not stored | article 50\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 50,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Because of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Revenue growth 70%\",\n     \"period\": \"next year\",\n     \"whose\": \"Keithen Drury (fool.com)\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-137\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > next year\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue growth: **70%** | basis: Company-wide revenue growth expectation cited by the author, unattributed to a named analyst or to company guidance | source as stored: Keithen Drury (fool.com) | period as stored: next year | said: 2026-09-26 | article 140\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 140,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"This could eat into Nvidia's business over the next few years, but with Nvidia expected to grow its revenue at a 70% pace during next year, I'd say it's doing just fine.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Sales growth 65.5%\",\n     \"period\": \"next year\",\n     \"whose\": \"Consensus analysts\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-138\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as someone else's forecast (estimate); the stored source is on each line > next year\",\n       \"line\": \"sales growth: **65.5%** | basis: consensus expectation | source as stored: Consensus analysts | period as stored: next year | said: 2026-09-29 | article 218\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 218,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Analysts continue to raise their earnings forecasts, with next year's estimate up 22% and revisions moving higher almost unanimously across other periods as well. Sales are expected to climb 88% this year and another 65.5% next year, while earnings are projected to rise 94% and 66%, respectively.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"b\",\n     \"value\": \"Earlier consensus revenue growth estimate 45%\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2028\",\n     \"whose\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own estimate; source as stored NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress); described as a prior Wall Street model\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-114\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as NVIDIA's own estimate (estimate) > FY2028\",\n       \"line\": \"Consensus revenue growth estimate: **45%** | basis: Wall Street model previously expected, per the article | source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) | period as stored: Fiscal 2028 | said: not stored | article 22\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 22,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Company revenue $178 billion\",\n     \"period\": \"First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026)\",\n     \"whose\": \"Nvidia, reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-029\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > first half of fiscal 2027 (ended july 26, 2026)\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue: **$178 billion** | basis: Company-wide revenue, up 96% year over year | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: First half of fiscal 2027 (ended July 26, 2026) | said: not stored | article 105\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 105,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"For now, Nvidia remains primarily focused on AI. In the first half of its fiscal 2027 (which ended July 26), its revenue rose by 96% year over year to $178 billion. Also, due to the relatively slower growth in its costs and expenses, its net income surged by 161% to $118 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Company revenue $96.2 billion, $96.22 billion, or $96 billion as stored\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal second quarter of 2027; Q2 fiscal 2027; fiscal Q2 2027; fiscal Q2 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026); Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026); Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26, 2026); Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 2026); second quarter of fiscal 2027\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA Corporation / NVIDIA Corp., reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-007\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Consolidated revenues: **$96.2 billion** | basis: Consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 35\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 35,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-017\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue: **$96 billion** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: not stored | article 232\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 232,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-018\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Revenue: **$96.2 billion** | stored from 7 articles, each with its own details: || article 37: basis: record; quarter ended July 26, 2026 | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 53: basis: company total | source as stored: Nvidia | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 (quarter ended July 26, 2026) | said: not stored || article 73: basis: Company total; up 106% from a year ago | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) | said: not stored || article 78: basis: Company total; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 121: basis: Company total revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26, 2026) | said: not stored || article 139: basis: up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially; company-reported | source as stored: NVIDIA Corp. | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 151: basis: as reported | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended July 2026) | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 37,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n          \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n          \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 53,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26) was another exceptional one. Revenue grew 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, an acceleration from the previous quarter, and the data center business contributed all but about $7 billion of the total. Profit kept up. Net income rose 126% year over year, to $59.7 billion.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 73,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\n          \"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 78,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n          \"Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 139,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 151,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-019\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"revenue: **$96.22 billion** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 46: basis: up 105.85% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: not stored || article 54: basis: Company total; up 105.8% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 46,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 54,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%.\",\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-026\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"revenues: **$96.22 billion** | basis: up 106% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: second quarter of fiscal 2027 | said: not stored | article 92\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 92,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-027\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"total revenue: **$96 billion** | basis: company total | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: not stored | article 115\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-028\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Total revenues: **$96 billion** | basis: Total company, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 122\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 122,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.\",\n          \"Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Data Center revenue $89 billion, $89.02 billion, or $89.023 billion as stored\",\n     \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027; fiscal second quarter of 2027; fiscal Q2 2027; Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26, 2026); second quarter of fiscal 2027; Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026); Fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corporation / NVIDIA Corp., reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-008\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenue: **$89 billion** | stored from 5 articles, each with its own details: || article 37: basis: segment; up 117% year-over-year; majority of total revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 78: basis: Data Center segment; up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 || article 115: basis: data center segment | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: not stored || article 121: basis: Data center segment, share of total revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26, 2026) | said: not stored || article 139: basis: up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA Corp. | period as stored: fiscal Q2 2027 | said: 2026-08-26\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 37,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\",\n          \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\",\n          \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 78,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\",\n          \"Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 115,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 121,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 139,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-009\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenue: **$89.023 billion** | basis: not stored | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 | said: not stored | article 46\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 46,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-011\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center revenues: **$89.02 billion** | basis: up 117% year over year; segment | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: second quarter of fiscal 2027 | said: not stored | article 92\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 92,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-012\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data center segment revenue: **$89 billion** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 73: basis: Segment; roughly 92.5% of total revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Q2 fiscal 2027 (quarter ended 26 July 2026) | said: not stored || article 122: basis: Data Center segment, up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA Corporation | period as stored: fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 73,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\",\n          \"Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 122,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.\",\n          \"Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-013\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > FY2027-Q2\",\n       \"line\": \"Data Center segment revenues: **$89 billion** | basis: Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Fiscal second quarter of 2027 | said: 2026-08-26 | article 35\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 35,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n          \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Company revenue $96.2 billion\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter; Latest reported quarter (unspecified)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-044\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"Total revenue: **$96.2 billion** | basis: Company-wide revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored | article 13\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 13,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-047\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter (unspecified)\",\n       \"line\": \"Total revenue: **$96.2 billion** | basis: Company total | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Latest reported quarter (unspecified) | said: not stored | article 39\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 39,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"group\": \"c\",\n     \"value\": \"Data Center revenue $89 billion\",\n     \"period\": \"latest reported quarter; Latest reported quarter (unspecified)\",\n     \"whose\": \"NVIDIA, reported\",\n     \"note\": null,\n     \"cited_lines\": [\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-041\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"data center revenue: **$89 billion** | stored from 2 articles, each with its own details: || article 114: basis: quarterly data center segment revenue, up 117% year over year | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored || article 116: basis: data center segment, latest quarter | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 114,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"According to the latest prints, Nvidia's quarterly data center revenue is now $89 billion -- up 117% year over year.\"\n         ]\n        },\n        {\n         \"article\": 116,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Revenue in its latest quarter also more than doubled, with almost all of it coming from data center customers, and management's outlook for the current quarter assumes no data center compute sales to China at all.\",\n          \"Nvidia's data center business brought in $89 billion in its latest quarter alone, and management guided the next quarter to $108 billion, excluding any China compute sales.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-042\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter\",\n       \"line\": \"Data-center revenue: **$89 billion** | basis: Data-center segment revenue, as reported in the company's quarterly results | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: latest reported quarter | said: not stored | article 13\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 13,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"That is an eye-catching demonstration, but the scale gap remains enormous: Nvidia's latest quarter generated $89 billion of data-center revenue out of $96.2 billion overall, meaning roughly 92.5% of sales came from the infrastructure businesses powering large-scale AI.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      },\n      {\n       \"id\": \"T-045\",\n       \"under\": \"Stored as reported by NVIDIA (actual) > latest reported quarter (unspecified)\",\n       \"line\": \"Data center revenue: **$89 billion** | basis: Data center segment revenue | source as stored: NVIDIA | period as stored: Latest reported quarter (unspecified) | said: not stored | article 39\",\n       \"stored_passages\": [\n        {\n         \"article\": 39,\n         \"passages\": [\n          \"Nvidia's latest quarter puts the scale of the contest in perspective: data centers generated $89 billion of its $96.2 billion in revenue, or roughly 92.5%.\"\n         ]\n        }\n       ]\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n }\n][\n {\n  \"article\": 16,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-22\",\n  \"publisher\": \"qz.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia stock is approaching its cheapest valuation in a decade despite booming profits\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia stock is trading at less than 17 times forward earnings, half the multiple it commanded in 2025, even as revenue and net income are expected to surge\\nNvidia $NVDA stock is trading near its lowest valuation in more than a decade, according to Bloomberg , even as the chipmaker's revenue and net income are each expected to grow by roughly 90% or more in its current fiscal year.\\nAt under 17 times forward earnings, the stock's multiple has been cut in half compared with 2025 — a period when Nvidia 's expansion was actually slower — and has fallen sharply from above 25 times expected profits just this past May, according to Bloomberg.\\n\\\"The stock has de-rated pretty significantly, which suggests a healthy dose of skepticism that the company's current earnings power is sustainable,\\\" Eli Horton, a TCW senior portfolio manager overseeing thematic and durable growth equities, told Bloomberg. \\\"The stock's performance is surprising, given the backdrop of incredible fundamentals, but it tells you the market is expecting less than what the consensus is currently estimating.\\\"\\nOne drag on the stock's valuation is a squeeze on profitability. Nvidia 's gross margin came in at 75% last quarter but analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show it sliding under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter. Rising costs for key components such as memory chips are a central factor.\\nCompetition is another concern. David Russell, global head of market strategy at TradeStation, pointed to the growing threat of customers building their own silicon, noting that Meta $META Platforms and Alphabet $GOOGL are among the major Nvidia buyers that have moved to design AI chips internally — a shift he believes will gradually erode Nvidia 's pricing power and market share. \\\"Multiples expand when companies are well positioned with potential to get better, and Nvidia doesn't offer that,\\\" Russell said.\\nNvidia stock has gained 22% so far in 2026, putting it second among the Magnificent Seven behind Apple $AAPL 's 25% advance — yet that performance looks modest against the broader chip sector. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange Semiconductor Index has surged nearly 76%, with Intel $INTC and Advanced Micro Devices both more than tripling; Nvidia sits near the bottom of the index's leaderboard, even as the index itself carries a valuation of 20 times forward earnings.\\nNvidia Chief Executive Officer Jensen Huang has pushed back on the market's assessment, calling the company \\\"the world's first and only growth value stock\\\" and describing Nvidia as \\\"incredibly misunderstood\\\" at a Goldman Sachs $GS technology conference earlier this month.\\nThe valuation discount follows a stretch of strong results. Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.\\nHorton said the current multiple looks favorable given that neither a pullback in AI infrastructure spending nor a major regulatory intervention appears imminent. \\\"This seems like a very favorable multiple to have as an entry point,\\\" he said.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 35,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-23\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n  \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 73,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"I'm Buying More Nvidia Stock Right Now. Here's Why.\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) has grown into the world's largest company, with a market cap of more than $5 trillion. Its graphics processing units (GPUs) are the gold standard for training and running high-level computing and artificial intelligence programs.\\nSome people may, however, look at Nvidia's stock price and think that the shine is coming off Jensen Huang's company. Shares are up 21% so far this year, which is fine, but that's not even close to the 239% gain in 2023, the 171% jump in 2024, or even last year's 39% increase. Meanwhile, stocks like Sandisk , Micron , and Intel are all up more than 200% this year and have become Wall Street darlings.\\nAre Nvidia's best days in the rearview mirror? Absolutely not. While the chipmaker may not replicate its massive 2023 gain, Nvidia remains an incredibly profitable company whose growth story is far from over. In fact, I think Nvidia is going to be putting up some wild numbers in the next two or three years, and that's why I'll be buying more Nvidia shares .\\nNvidia is still growing fast, despite its size\\nWhat stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\\nNet income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.\\n\\\"We're the only company in the world that creates and builds, offers an entire AI factory platform, a full-stack system. And customers can still mix and match,\\\" CEO Jensen Huang said. \\\"However, most companies just don't have the skills to do that or desire to do that. And so, there's an entire part of the market that we experience growth. There's sovereign AI, there're regional AIs, there're neoclouds, there're AI start-ups, there're enterprises, where we're seeing -- which represents about half of our business, and that's growing 100% a year.\\\"\\nNvidia's new product line is just getting started\\nNvidia's Hopper and Blackwell architectures have been enormously popular among hyperscalers and neoclouds building computing capacity to meet the growing demand for AI. But the company's Vera Rubin architecture, which uses NVLink chip-to-chip interconnects so its Rubin GPUs and Vera central processing units (CPUs) can share memory and improve efficiency, generates even more revenue. CFO Colette Kress said the Vera Rubin -- now in full production -- and full-stack AI platform has expanded the company's total addressable market from a range of $18 billion to $25 billion per gigawatt from Blackwell to $40 billion from Vera Rubin.\\nThat's one of the reasons why Huang has been so confident in his prediction -- which he made last year and recently reiterated -- that AI infrastructure spending would reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion by 2030. And speaking to reporters last week while in Scotland, Huang suggested that Nvidia would double its chip sales in the coming year.\\nIt's hard to get a group of people to agree on anything -- that's just human nature -- but Wall Street experts are nearly unanimous on Nvidia stock. Analysts at Rosenblatt have a \\\"Buy\\\" recommendation and a $390 price target, representing potential upside of 73%. Bernstein's analysts have a $400 price target and an \\\"Outperform\\\" rating, and Raymond James analysts have a whopping $515 price target and a \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating.\\nNvidia is expected to report another strong quarter when it next reports earnings (likely in late November), with the consensus revenue estimate of $109.0 billion, up 91.2% from the previous year. And more of the same is expected for next year, following the Vera Rubin ramp-up.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 92,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-24\",\n  \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n  \"title\": \"NVIDIA's $279B Supply Commitments: Can These Secure Its Growth Runway?\",\n  \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corporation 's NVDA increase in supply and capacity commitments signals confidence in sustained artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure demand. At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products. This gives NVIDIA greater visibility into component availability as it ramps up Blackwell and the next-generation Vera Rubin platform.\\nThe timing is important. NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion. The company expects third-quarter revenues of $108 billion (+/- 2%), indicating 89.5% year-over-year growth and a 12.2% sequential increase. Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\nThe supply commitments could help NVIDIA avoid shortages that might otherwise limit revenue growth. Securing memory and critical components in advance should support smoother production as AI customers expand data center deployments. NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.\\nHowever, the commitments create risks. NVIDIA could face higher costs or excess inventory if customer demand slows or product transitions occur faster than expected. The company warns that changing demand could make commitments difficult to reduce.\\nOverall, the $279 billion commitment provides NVIDIA with a stronger supply base for its AI growth plans, but execution and demand will determine how effectively that capacity converts into revenues. The Zacks Consensus Estimate for fiscal 2027 revenues is currently pegged at $406.05 billion, indicating an 88% year-over-year increase.\\nAMD and Intel Are Challenging NVIDIA in AI Race\\nAdvanced Micro Devices, Inc. AMD and Intel Corporation INTC are expanding their AI businesses.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices is broadening its data center AI opportunity from Instinct accelerators to the Helios rack-scale platform. In the second quarter of 2026, AMD's Data Center revenues rose 107% year over year to $6.7 billion, while Instinct sales more than doubled. Helios is in production, with initial shipments expected late in the third quarter and a larger ramp-up in the fourth quarter and 2027.\\nThe Helios platform has already garnered multiple large deals from big tech companies. Anthropic plans to deploy up to 2 gigawatts of MI450-series GPUs using the AMD Helios rack-scale platform, with the first gigawatt beginning in the first half of 2027. Microsoft plans to deploy Helios at scale on Azure. Advanced Micro Devices expects Data Center segment revenues to more than double year over year in 2027.\\nIntel is also gaining momentum in data center AI. Its second-quarter revenues rose 25% to $16.13 billion, while Data Center and AI revenues surged 59% to $6.26 billion. Intel also launched Xeon 6+ and expanded its open-source OpenVINO Physical AI framework, giving developers tools for AI and robotics applications.\\nIntel is building a broader AI infrastructure platform spanning CPUs, ASICs, graphics, networking and advanced packaging as customers design workload-specific systems. In the second quarter of 2026, purpose-built silicon revenues increased about 20% sequentially and nearly tripled year over year. During the last earnings call, management noted that purpose-built silicon revenues are approaching an estimated $2 billion run rate, and the company is targeting $4 billion in the near future.\\nShares of NVIDIA have risen around 20.8% year to date, underperforming the Zacks Computer and Technology sector's gain of 23.4%.\\nFrom a valuation standpoint, NVDA trades at a forward price-to-earnings ratio of 17.11, below the sector's average of 21.45.\\nThe Zacks Consensus Estimate for NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 and 2028 earnings implies a year-over-year increase of approximately 93.9% and 65.8%, respectively. Estimates for fiscal 2027 and 2028 have been revised upward over the past 30 days.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 121,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-25\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 166,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-27\",\n  \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n  \"title\": \"3 Reasons Why Nvidia Fits Warren Buffett's Investment Style\",\n  \"text\": \"While Warren Buffett and Berkshire Hathaway have never owned Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the chipmaker actually fits the investment profile of the stocks he loves to own. The semiconductor stock has a wide moat with a durable, compounding business model that is trading at an attractive valuation.\\nLet's dig deeper into why Nvidia should be considered a Buffett-style investment .\\nNvidia didn't stumble into becoming the king of artificial intelligence (AI) infrastructure by accident. It was a carefully orchestrated plan that took many years to pay off, but it resulted in a wide moat in the space.\\nIt all started with its CUDA software platform , which the company created to let developers program its powerful graphics processing units (GPUs) for tasks outside of their original purpose of speeding up graphics rendering in video games. Nvidia then took CUDA and planted it into universities and research labs that were doing early work on AI. That resulted in an entire generation of AI engineers trained on its software platform and most foundational AI code written using its software and optimized for its chips.\\nThose moves formed the wide moat that Nvidia sees in AI infrastructure today, particularly with regard to large language model (LLM) training.\\nHowever, the company did not stop there, designing a whole AI infrastructure ecosystem around CUDA and its GPUs. It developed its proprietary NVLink interconnect technology to help its chips act as a single unit, further widening its moat, and in 2020 it acquired next-gen networking company Mellanox, whose technology was arguably ahead of its time. Earlier this year, Nvidia \\\"acquired\\\" Groq to bring its language processing unit (LPU) technology into the CUDA ecosystem, putting Nvidia at the forefront of the fast-growing inference market.\\nNvidia has also made other smart moves to make sure its moat remains intact. It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips, and it also acquired important open-source AI systems in SchedMD and HuggingFace to move into the critical software layers used to orchestrate and distribute AI.\\nIn the span of two decades, Nvidia has gone from a video game chipmaker to a complete end-to-end infrastructure player with a wide and perhaps growing moat.\\nWarren Buffett championed Berkshire's investment in Alphabet and gave the go-ahead to invest an additional $10 billion in a private placement to help the company build out the AI infrastructure for its cloud computing unit. As such, he clearly believes in cloud computing economics.\\nWhen using Nvidia chips, cloud providers have typically been getting paybacks within two to three years, while the chips tend to have economic life spans of five to six years. That gives Nvidia a visible replacement cycle that is pretty similar to Buffett's top holding, Apple , with its iPhones and computers.\\nMeanwhile, the great thing about AI versus past huge tech trends, like the internet and smartphones, is that its demand can essentially be limitless if it continues to provide a strong economic payback, not bound by human consumption. Combined with a well-defined replacement cycle, Nvidia should have a strong compounding business even when the AI frenzy starts to calm down.\\nEven with great businesses, Buffett isn't willing to pay any price. He wants to buy stocks when they are trading below their intrinsic value, and Nvidia can certainly make that argument.\\nThe stock trades at a forward P/E of 24 times fiscal 2027 (ending January 2027) and just above 14 times the fiscal 2028 consensus. This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.\\nWhile revenue growth will eventually slow, Nvidia is a stock worth a lot more than it is currently valued.\\nBy Geoffrey Seiler – Sep 27, 2026 at 3:55AM EST\"\n },\n {\n  \"article\": 222,\n  \"day\": \"2026-09-28\",\n  \"publisher\": \"gurufocus.com\",\n  \"title\": \"Nvidia Just Put a Record $150 Billion Behind Its Own Stock\",\n  \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA , Financials ) is putting another $150 billion behind its own stock at a time when its valuation looks unusually low by historical standards.\\nThe chipmaker increased its share-repurchase authorization by a record $150 billion, lifting its remaining buyback capacity to $235 billion. Nvidia expects to use that authorization through fiscal 2028.\\nThe new authorization even tops Apple's $110 billion buyback approval in 2024.\\nWhat's more unusual is the timing.\\nNvidia trades at roughly 16.5 times 12-month forward earnings, its lowest multiple since January 2015 and well below its 15-year average of 30 times, according to LSEG data cited by Reuters.\\nThe lower multiple comes despite Nvidia forecasting roughly 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 as demand for AI training and inference continues to drive its business.\\nCEO Jensen Huang said Nvidia's cash generation allows the company to keep investing in AI technology while returning capital to shareholders.\\nNvidia ended its July quarter with $22.44 billion in cash and cash equivalents. The company had previously announced an $80 billion buyback in May.\\nShares rose nearly 2% Monday and have gained more than 20% this year through Friday.\\nThe $235 billion authorization now gives Nvidia substantial room to repurchase shares through fiscal 2028, even as investors debate how long today's pace of AI spending can continue.\"\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-compare-packet-m02.json\\\"))[0];\nfor s, entries in p[\\\"sets\\\"].items():\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",s)\n for e in entries:\n  print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",e[\\\"value\\\"],\\\"|\\\",e[\\\"period\\\"],\\\"|\\\",e[\\\"whose\\\"],\\\"|\\\",e.get(\\\"note\\\"))\n  for l in e[\\\"cited_lines\\\"]: print(\\\" \\\",l[\\\"id\\\"],[(x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"passages\\\"]) for x in l[\\\"stored_passages\\\"]])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/record-3/week-compare-packet-m02.json\\\"))[0];\nfor s, entries in p[\\\"sets\\\"].items():\n print(\\\"\\\\n\\\",s)\n for e in entries:\n  print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",e[\\\"value\\\"],\\\"|\\\",e[\\\"period\\\"],\\\"|\\\",e[\\\"whose\\\"],\\\"|\\\",e.get(\\\"note\\\"))\n  for l in e[\\\"cited_lines\\\"]: print(\\\" \\\",l[\\\"id\\\"],[(x[\\\"article\\\"],x[\\\"passages\\\"]) for x in l[\\\"stored_passages\\\"]])'\"","aggregated_output":"\n S1\nANSWER Revenue growth about 70% (stored as 70%, about 70%, roughly 70%, approximately 70%); company-wide, preliminary, described as supply-constrained; made with the fiscal 2027 second-quarter results, first time NVIDIA guided a full year ahead; T-099 adds growth could double if commodity supply constraints were overcome. A growth rate only: no dollar level is stored | fiscal 2028 ('Fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)' in T-095) | NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) / NVIDIA management (CEO) in T-094's article 98; said late August 2026) | None\n  T-093 [(16, ['Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.']), (201, ['the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028)'])]\n  T-094 [(43, ['Last month, Nvidia posted a market-beating Q2 and issued bullish guidance for about 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which makes its current P/E multiple of about 24x appear rather attractive.']), (92, ['NVIDIA also expects fiscal 2028 revenue growth of about 70%, although supply constraints could limit how quickly it meets demand.']), (98, ['For fiscal 2028, management has guided about 70% revenue growth, the first time it has guided a full year ahead. That guide does not depend on finding more demand. The CEO says demand is much greater than 70%, and that supply is what holds the guide there.']), (177, ['Management’s preliminary outlook is for revenue to grow about 70% in fiscal 2028. Management called that outlook supply-constrained. Demand without that limit would be a lot higher, management said.'])]\n  T-095 [(121, ['And on the company\\'s late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.']), (222, ['The lower multiple comes despite Nvidia forecasting roughly 70% revenue growth for fiscal 2028 as demand for AI training and inference continues to drive its business.'])]\n  T-096 [(54, ['Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%.', \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\"])]\n  T-097 [(22, ['while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled'])]\n  T-098 [(53, [\"On the company's earnings call in late August, chief financial officer Colette Kress went a whole year further.\", '\"We expect to grow revenue by approximately 70% in fiscal 2028,\" Kress said. \"This is a supply-constrained outlook.\"'])]\n  T-099 [(45, ['In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips.', \"But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware.\", \"On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 2027 earnings call , Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said:\", 'Incredibly, we are seeing demand acceleration even at our scale. Customer forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply-constrained.'])]\nANSWER Revenue growth 70% (stored as 70%, roughly 70%, around 70%), the same value stored under relative or missing period labels | stored relatively: 'next fiscal year' (T-105, T-106); 'next year' (T-108); 'next year (2027)', no fiscal/calendar basis specified (T-109); period not stored (T-113) | NVIDIA, its own outlook (guidance section; source as stored NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA management; T-113's basis: Nvidia forecast as described by Morningstar) | None\n  T-105 [(55, ['management told investors to expect roughly 70% revenue growth in the next fiscal year'])]\n  T-106 [(166, ['This is for a company with about 75% gross margins that grew its revenue by more than 100% last quarter and projected that it would increase its revenue by around 70% next fiscal year.'])]\n  T-108 [(162, ['Nvidia projects this supply/demand situation will continue, contributing to a 70% revenue growth forecast for next year.'])]\n  T-109 [(158, [\"Next year, its management team believes the company will grow revenue at a 70% pace. If you've followed Nvidia throughout the AI race, you know that management often sandbags its guidance, so the real growth rate is likely higher than this.\"])]\n  T-113 [(32, ['Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.'])]\nANSWER Chip sales: double (NVIDIA chip sales; forecast attributed to CEO Jensen Huang) | next year | Jensen Huang, as the stored source; the section stores it as someone else's own outlook (guidance), while the line's basis calls him NVIDIA's CEO | None\n  T-121 [(99, ['Huang, who said that Nvidia would double its chip sales next year'])]\nANSWER Consensus sales estimate $671.95 billion (up 65.5% on the current year) | Next fiscal year | Zacks Consensus Estimate (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: Zacks Investment Research (Zacks Consensus Estimate); said 2026-09-22) | None\n  T-134 [(11, ['For the current and next fiscal years, $406.05 billion and $671.95 billion estimates indicate +88% and +65.5% changes, respectively.'])]\nANSWER Sales growth 65.5% (consensus expectation) | next year | Consensus analysts (someone else's forecast section; source as stored: Consensus analysts; said 2026-09-29) | None\n  T-138 [(218, [\"Analysts continue to raise their earnings forecasts, with next year's estimate up 22% and revisions moving higher almost unanimously across other periods as well. Sales are expected to climb 88% this year and another 65.5% next year, while earnings are projected to rise 94% and 66%, respectively.\"])]\nANSWER Revenue growth 66% (company-wide) | Next year (fiscal 2028, ends January 2028) | Wall Street consensus (analysts) (someone else's forecast section) | None\n  T-139 [(50, ['Because of its central position in the AI boom, Nvidia is growing much faster than the S&P 500. Wall Street expects the company to grow revenue by 91% this year and 66% next year.'])]\nANSWER Consensus revenue growth estimate 45%: what the Wall Street model previously expected, before the company's about-70% projection | Fiscal 2028 | Wall Street consensus per the line's basis (T-093 also says 'compared with 45% analysts had expected at the time'); the section and the stored source give it as NVIDIA's own estimate (source as stored: NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)) | None\n  T-114 [(22, ['while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled'])]\n  T-093 [(16, ['Nvidia stock climbed roughly 7% after its second-quarter earnings , when revenue more than doubled and the company projected 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — well above the 45% analysts had expected at the time.']), (201, ['the company reported second-quarter financial results that topped Wall Street expectations and projected 70% revenue growth for the next year (fiscal 2028)'])]\nANSWER Revenue growth 70% (company-wide expectation cited by the author, unattributed to a named analyst or to company guidance) | next year | Keithen Drury (fool.com) (someone else's forecast section; said 2026-09-26) | None\n  T-137 [(140, [\"This could eat into Nvidia's business over the next few years, but with Nvidia expected to grow its revenue at a 70% pace during next year, I'd say it's doing just fine.\"])]\nANSWER Share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing: roughly 25% (the article's expectation for fiscal 2028 revenue; a share, not a revenue estimate) | Fiscal 2028 | barchart.com (article analysis) (someone else's forecast section; said 2026-09-22) | None\n  T-126 [(22, ['Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.'])]\nANSWER Revenue $96.2 billion (also stored as $96.22 billion and $96 billion), up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially: the latest reported quarter; fiscal 2027, the year fiscal 2028 growth is measured from, is not stored as reported | Q2 fiscal 2027 (also stored as 'Fiscal second quarter of 2027', 'fiscal Q2 2027', quarter ended July 26, 2026) | NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA / Nvidia / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation; said 2026-08-26) | None\n  T-007 [(35, [\"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\", \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"])]\n  T-017 [(232, ['Management said fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96 billion more than doubled from a year earlier. Growth also sped up for the fourth quarter in a row.'])]\n  T-018 [(37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"]), (53, [\"Nvidia's fiscal 2027 second quarter (ended July 26) was another exceptional one. Revenue grew 106% year over year to $96.2 billion, an acceleration from the previous quarter, and the data center business contributed all but about $7 billion of the total. Profit kept up. Net income rose 126% year over year, to $59.7 billion.\"]), (73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (78, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\", 'Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.', \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (121, [\"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"]), (139, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\", \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (151, ['Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.'])]\n  T-019 [(46, ['Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.']), (54, ['Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue was $96.22 billion, up 105.8% year over year, with a Q3 guide of $108 billion at the midpoint and a preliminary fiscal 2028 growth expectation of approximately 70%.', \"Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue surged 106% year over year to $96B, making the director's trim a portfolio move, not a business warning.\"])]\n  T-020 [(37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"]), (151, ['Nvidia reported exceptional financial results in the second quarter of fiscal 2027, which ended in July 2026. Revenue increased 106% to $96.2 billion, gross margin expanded 2.5 percentage points, and non-GAAP net income rose 120% to $2.22 per diluted share.'])]\n  T-021 [(37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])]\n  T-026 [(92, ['NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion.'])]\n  T-027 [(115, ['NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.'])]\n  T-028 [(122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s gross margin rose to 75% from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased. NVIDIA’s GAAP operating income has more than doubled year over year, implying significant operating leverage.'])]\nANSWER Data Center revenue $89 billion (also stored as $89.02 billion and $89.023 billion), up 117% year over year; roughly 92.5% of total revenue | Q2 fiscal 2027 (also stored as 'fiscal second quarter of 2027', 'fiscal Q2 2027') | NVIDIA, reported (actual section; source as stored NVIDIA / NVIDIA Corp. / NVIDIA Corporation; said 2026-08-26) | None\n  T-008 [(37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"]), (78, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release.\", 'Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.', \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"]), (115, ['NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total.']), (121, [\"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"]), (139, [\"NVIDIA's revenue growth has been phenomenal, reaching $96.2 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 26 press release. Data Center remained the primary growth engine, with its revenues jumping 117% year over year to $89 billion, indicating continued strength in AI infrastructure demand.\", \"NVIDIA's gross margin reached 75%, up from 72.4% a year ago, while both GAAP and non-GAAP net income increased, demonstrating the company's strong profitability. GAAP operating income more than doubled year over year, underscoring significant operating leverage.\"])]\n  T-009 [(46, ['Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.'])]\n  T-010 [(37, [\"Nvidia delivered another blockbuster quarter, reporting its fiscal second-quarter 2027 results on Aug. 26, for the quarter ended July 26. Revenue reached a record $96.2 billion, up 106% year-over-year (YoY) from $46.7 billion, and increased 18% sequentially. The result also comfortably exceeded Nvidia's prior guidance of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%.\", \"The company's profitability expanded even faster. On a non-GAAP basis, net income climbed 118% to $54 billion, versus $24.8 billion in Q2 fiscal 2026, while adjusted EPS jumped 120% to $2.22, exceeding expectations. Non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5%. Operating income surged 124% YoY to $63.7 billion.\", \"Nvidia's Data Center segment remained the primary growth engine, generating $89 billion in revenue, up 117% YoY from the prior-year quarter and representing the majority of total company revenue. The performance reflects continued demand for Nvidia's accelerated-computing platforms as hyperscalers and AI companies expand infrastructure.\"])]\n  T-011 [(92, ['NVIDIA generated $96.22 billion in second-quarter fiscal 2027 revenues, up 106% year over year, while Data Center revenues surged 117% to $89.02 billion.'])]\n  T-012 [(73, [\"What stands out in Nvidia's most recent earnings report is that the company is still growing at a triple-digit rate. Revenue in the second quarter of fiscal 2027 (ending July 26, 2026) was $96.2 billion, up 106% from a year ago. Of that, $89 billion, or roughly 92.5% of the company's total revenue, came from its data center segment.\", 'Net income was $59.68 billion, up 126% from a year ago, and earnings per share of $2.46 were up 128% from last year. Nvidia is very profitable -- its operating expenses totaled only $8.4 billion for the quarter, and its gross margin was an impressive 75%.']), (122, ['NVIDIA’s Data Center segment remained its key growth driver, with revenues surging 117% year over year to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the company’s Aug. 26 press release. Total revenues reached an exceptional $96 billion, representing a 106% year-over-year and 18% sequential jump.', 'Along with revenue growth, the company’s profitability has also strengthened. NVIDIA’s 